 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Reset timing",
   "page": 6,
   "text": "RESET is driven low while VCC is under the threshold trip voltage. When VCC rises past the threshold again, RESET is held low for the reset timeout period, 140 ms at least (240 ms typical, 400 ms maximum), and then released high. Below the threshold the falling-VCC response is typically 20 µs for a 100 mV overdrive."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Manual reset",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "A low level on MR asserts RESET for as long as MR stays low and for the reset timeout after it returns high. MR is pulled up internally (10 to 75 kΩ, 20 kΩ typical), so an unused MR is left open. A pulse of 10 µs minimum is recognised; pulses no longer than 100 ns typically are not. The MR-to-RESET delay is 0.5 µs typical."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Supply transients",
   "page": 6,
   "text": "Short negative-going VCC transients are ignored: a dip of 4 µs or less at 100 mV amplitude typically causes no false reset. The longest dip that causes no reset shortens as the overdrive VTH - VCC grows."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Valid reset with VCC under 1.0 V",
   "page": 6,
   "text": "The output state is guaranteed for VCC down to 1.0 V. To keep RESET at a known level below 1.0 V, a 100 kΩ pull-down from RESET to GND is recommended; its value is not critical."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Bi-directional reset pins",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "A processor or controller whose reset pin is bi-directional is connected through a 4.7 kΩ resistor in series between the CAT811 RESET output and that pin."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Thresholds",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "The part is built on onsemi's floating-gate analog EEPROM technology, which makes any custom threshold value possible. Seven standard thresholds are offered for +5.0 V, +3.3 V, +3.0 V and +2.5 V systems, giving monitoring of +5.0 V at ±5 %, ±10 % and ±20 % and of +3.3 V at ±5 % and ±10 %. The threshold tempco is 30 ppm/°C typical."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Compatibility",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "The CAT811 drops in for the MAX811 where the application stays within -40 °C to +85 °C."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Applications",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Named uses: laptops, servers, computers, cable modems, wireless equipment, embedded controllers, power meters, white goods, instruments, PDAs and other handheld devices."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Marking",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "The top of the package carries XXXM with a Pb-free dot: XXX is the device code (VEA for every CAT811 threshold), M is the month code, and the Pb-free microdot may sit in either position."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Status",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "The CAT811 is discontinued and not recommended for new designs."
  }
 ],
